# /{{word}} — this harness's word for running it

Scaffolded once by /the-world:start, so that running this harness is a real command here
rather than something only the setup session knew. Run everything from this harness's
directory. Keep speaking in {{universe_name}}'s vocabulary, and keep commands, pids, paths
and "not running" exact.

## Read before acting

Every request starts with

```bash
uv run python bin/ops-kit.py --check
```

It writes nothing. It says whether the launcher scripts exist and were rendered for this
folder, whether `.venv` was made here, whether the watchdog is registered and which
script it runs, and whether the harness is running. Below that it prints either this
harness's exact commands (`start`, `status`, `restart`, `logs`, `stop`, `register`,
`unregister`, `watchdog`, `take-down`) or, with exit 2, a numbered fix. Use the commands
it prints rather than ones you remember: they carry this harness's script name and this
machine's service manager (launchd, systemd or Task Scheduler).

When the scripts exist and are current, also run the `status` command before you answer
or act. Report what the output says is running: the pid and uptime, the console writer,
the watchdog's state, the last lines. Never say what is running from memory or from this
conversation.

## No scripts yet

`scripts: none rendered` means the harness runs only while someone keeps
`uv run python harness.py` open in a terminal. To start it in the background, restart it
from here, or keep it alive, render the scripts first:

```bash
uv run python bin/ops-kit.py
```

If Slack is connected, add `--notify --prefix <PREFIX>` so restarts are posted to the
harness's channel. Find the prefix from the key names, never the values:
`python3 -I world/env.py --keys env/harness.env` lists names such as
`<PREFIX>_SLACK_BOT_TOKEN`. Never print or read back `env/harness.env` itself. If ops-kit
refuses `--notify` because a value is missing, render without it and say so. Rendering
writes only `scripts/`; it starts and registers nothing. Before the first `up`, ask the
person to stop any `harness.py` they still run in a terminal, since a second copy beside
it is refused.

## What people ask, and what you run

| They say | You run |
|---|---|
| Start it (켜줘) | `start`, then `status` |
| Stop it for a while (잠깐 꺼줘) | `stop`. The watchdog leaves it down until the next `start`. |
| Restart it (재시작) | `restart`, then `status` |
| How is it (상태) | `--check`, then `status`, and `watchdog` when one is registered |
| Show its output | the last lines from `status`; `logs` follows it live, so give that one to the person |
| Keep it alive, even after a reboot (재부팅해도 살아있게) | render the scripts if missing; show `register`; once the person ran it, `--check` until the watchdog reads loaded or enabled, then `start` if it is not running |
| Take it down for good (완전히 꺼줘) | show `unregister` and wait until `--check` no longer reads it as registered; then `stop` |

The order for taking it down is the kit's rule: remove the watchdog first, then stop.

## The watchdog belongs to the person

`register` and `unregister` change what this machine starts at login (`launchctl
bootstrap` / `bootout`, `systemctl --user enable` / `disable`, `schtasks /Create` /
`/Delete`). Show them and let the person run them, then confirm with `--check`. Run them
yourself only when the person asks you to in so many words. Starting, stopping and
restarting with the harness's own script are yours to do when asked.

## After the folder moved

The scripts, `.venv`, and a registered watchdog all hold the path they were made at. When
`--check` exits 2 it prints the fix in order: unregister the old watchdog, stop what is
running, recreate `.venv`, re-render with `--force` keeping the same `--name` and flags,
register again, start. Show the whole list and run it after the person says yes, leaving
the watchdog lines to them as above. Re-run `--check` afterwards and report what it says.
If the old folder still holds a harness, `--check` calls this one a copy: the original keeps
its scripts, watchdog and process, and the fix only renders this folder under its own name.
A venv-only finding needs no hurry: the harness still runs through the venv's python, so
offer to recreate it at a quiet moment.

`docs/operations.md` has the rest: logs, the console writer, Windows differences.

